The lens looks again bigger when it found a code it could not read

Found by pointing decodeImage at a real video: a droplet loop that went over SMS
and came back at 192x192. The data was intact — OpenCV read 95% of the frames and
reassembled the whole 16,358-byte payload from that file. Our lens read 0 of 261.

Blowing the same file up 4x nearest-neighbour, which adds no information
whatsoever, took our lens from 0% to 98.9% and it caught and verified the payload.
So this was never damage. It was scale.

Diagnosis: findFinders locates the code fine at 2.6 px/module — 19 candidates on
the failing frames. What breaks is downstream: mSize is ~2.6, dimEst divides
distances by it, and the one-sample-per-module read then lands on the wrong side
of module boundaries. Neither smallness nor softness alone does it — the torture
row's "scale 2" case is crisp and passes today, and a blurred scale-6 code passes
too. It takes both, which is exactly what somebody else's codec hands you.

The change: when the loop ends having LOCATED a code (finders resolved to a trio)
whose modules are under 8px, crop to that code, blow the crop up nearest-neighbour
to ~10 px/module, and look once more. Returned geometry is mapped back to original
coordinates, with rescaledBy set so callers can tell.

Measured 7px -> 72%, 8.8px -> 92%, 10.5px -> 99% on identical bits, which is where
the 10px target comes from.

Two things keep the cost honest:
- It only fires when finders were already found and decode still failed. A frame
  with no code in it does no extra work at all, which is what matters at camera
  frame rates.
- It crops to the code before scaling, so memory is proportional to the code and
  not the frame. A naive whole-frame 4x on a 1280x720 camera buffer would be
  59MB per attempt; this is a few hundred KB.

Tests: added case 6 to qr-decode.test.mjs — small AND soft (3 px/module, edges
blurred) now reads, asserts rescaledBy fired, and asserts a comfortable code never
pays for the retry. qr-decode, qr-encode, carrier, fountain, transfer, sneakernet
and meet-carrier suites all pass.

Worth knowing separately, not changed here: a standalone carrierSession only
reports complete when every member it has SEEN is done (isComplete, the no-layout
branch). Point a camera at a page running nine loops and the session never says
complete until the slowest one lands, even though eight are sitting there
finished. present[].done is the per-bottle signal; anything with several emitters
on screen should read that instead. Flagging rather than touching it, since the
set semantics are presumably deliberate for layout-attested sets.

The harness that found this is catch-video.mjs, in the FCCN-ANTIBODY workspace
under renders/lucky-sevens/_degradation-study/ (not a repo, so it cannot come
along in this PR). It streams a video through ffmpeg into decodeImage and
carrierSession and reports what got caught and when.
